<!DOCTYPE html>
<!-- 1. LANGUAGE ATTRIBUTE: Tells screen readers which voice/pronunciation rules to use -->
<html lang="en">

<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Accessible HTML5 Page Structure</title>
    <style>
        body {
            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
            line-height: 1.6;
            max-width: 800px;
            margin: 0 auto;
            padding: 0 16px;
        }

        /* 2. SKIP LINK STYLING: Hidden off-screen until focused via keyboard (Tab key) */
        .skip-link {
            position: absolute;
            top: -50px;
            left: 16px;
            background-color: #0f172a;
            color: #ffffff;
            padding: 10px 16px;
            text-decoration: none;
            font-weight: bold;
            border-radius: 4px;
            z-index: 100;
        }

        .skip-link:focus {
            top: 16px; /* Slides into view when the user presses Tab */
        }

        nav ul {
            list-style: none;
            padding: 0;
            display: flex;
            gap: 16px;
            background-color: #f1f5f9;
            padding: 12px;
        }

        /* Visible focus outline for keyboard navigation */
        a:focus, button:focus, input:focus {
            outline: 3px solid #0284c7;
            outline-offset: 2px;
        }
    </style>
</head>

<body>

    <!-- 2. SKIP LINK: Must be the very first focusable element inside <body> -->
    <a href="#main-content" class="skip-link">Skip to main content</a>

    <header>
        <!-- ARIA LABEL ON LANDMARK: Distinguishes this navigation bar from any footer nav -->
        <nav aria-label="Primary Navigation">
            <ul>
                <li><a href="#articles">Articles</a></li>
                <li><a href="#tutorials">Tutorials</a></li>
                <li><a href="#about">About</a></li>
            </ul>
        </nav>
    </header>

    <!-- Target for the skip link; tabindex="-1" ensures programmatic focus works in all browsers -->
    <main id="main-content" tabindex="-1">

        <!-- 3. PROPER HEADING HIERARCHY: Single <h1> followed sequentially by <h2> and <h3> -->
        <h1>Web Accessibility Fundamentals</h1>
        <p>Building inclusive web pages ensures everyone—including screen reader and keyboard users—can navigate your content.</p>

        <section id="articles">
            <h2>1. Descriptive Alt Text</h2>
            
            <!-- 4. ALT TEXT: Describes the meaning/context of the image, not "image of..." -->
            <figure>
                <img src="https://picsum.photos/id/1/600/250" 
                     alt="Developer typing HTML code on a laptop at a wooden desk" 
                     width="600" 
                     height="250">
                <figcaption>Meaningful alt text conveys visual context to screen readers.</figcaption>
            </figure>

            <!-- Decorative images use an empty alt="" so screen readers skip them -->
            <img src="https://via.placeholder.com/600x10/cbd5e1/cbd5e1" alt="">
        </section>

        <section id="tutorials">
            <h2>2. ARIA Labels for Non-Text Controls</h2>
            <p>When a button or input lacks visible text, use <code>aria-label</code> or <code>aria-labelledby</code>:</p>

            <!-- ARIA LABEL ON FORM & BUTTON: Provides accessible names for icon-only controls -->
            <form role="search" aria-label="Site Search">
                <input type="search" aria-label="Search articles" placeholder="Search...">
                <button type="submit" aria-label="Submit search">&#128269;</button>
            </form>

            <h3>Sub-section Heading (h3)</h3>
            <p>Notice how headings step down logically from <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> &rarr; <code>&lt;h2&gt;</code> &rarr; <code>&lt;h3&gt;</code> without skipping levels.</p>
        </section>

    </main>

    <footer>
        <p>&copy; 2026 Accessible Web Demo</p>
    </footer>

</body>

